  {
    "pkheadingid": 769,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 706,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 121579,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 369,
        "headingId": 706,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Spleen</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-02-28T17:00:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 770,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 707,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 121580,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 369,
        "headingId": 707,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>SANT is often asymptomatic, with 50&ndash;90% of cases detected incidentally by imaging studies. Some patients have symptoms, which are usually related to mass effects (abdominal discomfort, palpable mass) or hypersplenism (cytopenia, portal hypertension) {15371942; 18643852; 22858789; 30626827; 32063479; 34280388}. Growth retardation and splenic rupture have been rarely reported in children {19863210; 31214299}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-02-28T17:00:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 771,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 708,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 121581,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 369,
        "headingId": 708,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>SANT can occur in all age groups but most commonly affects middle-aged adults (median age: 46&nbsp;years), and it has a female predilection (M:F&nbsp;ratio: 1:1.25&ndash;1.63) {22858789; 30626827}. The incidence of SANT is unknown. SANT has been detected in as many as 15% of splenectomy specimens in some studies {34512908}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-02-28T17:00:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 772,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 709,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 121582,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 369,
        "headingId": 709,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>The etiology is unknown. Roles for IgG4-related disease or EBV infection have been previously postulated, but these suggestions are not supported by current evidence {27526296; 30626827; 32600744}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-02-28T17:00:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 773,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 710,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 121583,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 369,
        "headingId": 710,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>It has been hypothesized that fibrotic stromal proliferation from diverse causes may disrupt the local microcirculation, leading to transformation of red pulp into angiomatoid nodules {15371942; 18643852}. Assessment of clonality using the human androgen receptor (HUMARA) assay suggested that SANT is a polyclonal, reactive lesion {27526296}, but a recent study showed <em>CTNNB1</em> exon&nbsp;3 deletions in SANT, suggesting that it is a benign neoplasm {33650044}. Additional research is needed.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-02-28T17:00:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 780,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 717,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 121584,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 369,
        "headingId": 717,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>SANT generally presents as a single, well-circumscribed, round to bosselated mass (mean diameter: 50&nbsp;mm), or it may be composed of multiple such lesions (in 5&ndash;13% of cases) {26159169; 30626827}. There are multiple brown-red nodules, more numerous at the periphery of the lesion, delineated by intervening whitish fibrotic septa that converge to form a central stellate scar. These features manifest as a spoke-and-wheel pattern on enhanced CT or MRI {33495124}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-02-28T17:00:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 774,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 711,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 121585,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 369,
        "headingId": 711,
        "headingText": "<p>Round or convoluted angiomatoid nodules, which can coalesce, are surrounded by concentric layers of collagen or fibrinoid material. The nodules are composed of slit-like, ectatic, or irregularly shaped vascular channels lined by attenuated or plump endothelial cells, with abundant red blood cells within or outside the vascular lumina. No cytological atypia or significant mitotic activity is found. The intervening stroma is densely fibrotic or composed of fibromyxoid tissue containing variable numbers of lymphocytes, plasma cells, eosinophils, and haemosiderin-laden macrophages. Immunohistochemical studies show a variable mixture of blood vessels as seen in splenic red pulp, including sinusoids (CD31+, CD8+, CD34&minus;), capillaries (CD31+, CD8&minus;, CD34+), and veins (CD31+, CD8&minus;, CD34&minus;).</p>\r\n\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n\r\n<p>Splenic hamartoma lacks discrete angiomatoid nodules. Splenic haemangioma comprises a single type of vessels, namely capillaries (CD31+, CD8&minus;, CD34+).</p>\r\n&nbsp;",
        "timestamp": "2024-02-28T17:00:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
